# Your rule content

AI Development Rules for Pool XL (KJ_Game_Suite)
Visuals vs. Physics: Remember that the visual table elements (border, rails, felt) drawn on the canvas might have different dimensions than the Matter.js physics boundaries (walls/cushions).
Action: Ensure physics walls (wallTop, wallBottom, etc.) align precisely with the inner edge of the playable felt area (defined by PLAYFIELD_X, PLAYFIELD_Y, PLAYFIELD_WIDTH, PLAYFIELD_HEIGHT), not the visual outer border. Balls should only interact physically within this playfield.
Pocket Placement & Detection:
Visuals: Pockets should be drawn overlapping the visual rails for realism. Their center coordinates (pockets array) should reflect this visual placement (currently at the playfield corners).
Physics: The pocket detection logic in the gameLoop uses POCKET_RADIUS. Ensure this radius is appropriate for capturing balls near the visual pocket centers.
Drawing Order Matters: Render canvas elements in the correct sequence for layering:
Outer Border (drawTable)
Rails (drawTable)
Felt (drawTable)
Pockets (drawPockets called within drawTable)
Balls (drawBall called for each ball in gameLoop)
UI Elements (Cue stick, power bar, indicator, guideline - drawn last in gameLoop when gamePhase === 'aiming')
Physics Tuning:
Use cushionOptions.restitution to control cushion bounciness (currently 0.9).
Use ballOptions.restitution, friction, frictionAir, and frictionStatic to control ball behavior. frictionAir is particularly important for how quickly balls slow down.
When applying force (especially for shooting/spin), use Matter.Body.applyForce at a specific point, not just setVelocity. Use scaling factors like FORCE_SCALING to translate input power into appropriate force magnitudes.
Coordinate Systems: Be mindful of canvas coordinates (pixels, origin top-left) for drawing and Matter.js world coordinates for physics. Use getMousePos for accurate mouse mapping.
Game State: Strictly adhere to gamePhase ('aiming', 'simulating') to control input handling, physics updates (Engine.update), and UI visibility. Only update the engine when simulationRunning is true.
Ball Data: Use ballDataMap (mapping body.id to { number, color, isSolid }) to store and retrieve custom ball properties. Do not try to store this directly on Matter.js body objects.
Constants: Use the defined constants (e.g., BORDER_SIZE, RAIL_SIZE, PLAYFIELD_X/Y/WIDTH/HEIGHT, POCKET_RADIUS, BALL_RADIUS) for calculations related to table geometry, physics, and drawing to maintain consistency.

